=========================================================
14. std::decay
==============

Converts a type to the form used when passing by value.

Example

using T1 = decay<int&>::type;

using T2 = decay<const int>::type;

Results

T1 = int

T2 = int

Used heavily in STL and template libraries.

=========================================================
15. std::enable_if (C++11)
==========================

Used to enable or disable functions at compile time based on conditions.

Example

template<typename T>
typename enable_if<is_integral<T>::value, void>::type
print(T value)
{
    cout << value << endl;
}

Usage

print(10);

Works

--------------------------------

print(3.14);

Compilation Error

Reason

The function is enabled only for integral types.

=========================================================
16. std::conditional
====================

Compile-time if-else.

Syntax

conditional<condition, TrueType, FalseType>::type

Example

using T = conditional<true,int,double>::type;

Result

T = int

=========================================================
17. std::common_type
====================

Finds a common type between multiple types.

Example

using T = common_type<int,double>::type;

Result

double

=========================================================
18. if constexpr (C++17)
========================

Type Traits are often used with if constexpr.

Example

template<typename T>
void check()
{
    if constexpr(is_integral<T>::value)
    {
        cout << "Integral";
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Not Integral";
    }
}

Usage

check<int>();

check<double>();

Output

Integral

Not Integral

